     "text": "Combines Soothsayer + Witch · Aligned with Soothsayer, Witch"
    },
    {
     "name": "Sonancer",
     "text": "Combines Chronomancer + Enchanter · Aligned with Chronomancer, Enchanter"
    },
    {
     "name": "Spiritualist",
     "text": "Combines Necromancer + Thaumaturge · Aligned with Necromancer, Thaumaturge"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Lost Spells",
   "blurb": "",
   "entries": [
    {
     "name": "Alignment / Meteor Strike / Misalignment / Shape Starfire Elemental / Starfall / Starfire Bolt",
     "tag": "Astromancer",
     "text": "Health-on-cast synergy, a +7 shooting blast, self-damaging spellstorm, summonable elemental, area mines, and a +4 elemental bolt."
    },
    {
     "name": "Break Armour / Collapse / Fracture / Implode-Explode / Misstep / Whiplash",
     "tag": "Distortionist",
     "text": "Armour destruction, a +4 terrain-based blast, a combat-only free attack, mass pull/push, pre-game enemy reposition, and a movement teleport."
    },
    {
     "name": "Blood Wager / Fickle Finger / Mischance / Scatter / Serendipity / True Gold",
     "tag": "Fatecaster",
     "text": "Coin-flip full heal or half-Health loss, action-count randomizer, forced disadvantage/advantage rolls, treasure-token shove, and a treasure reroll."
    },
    {
     "name": "Charm / Imbue Instrument / Humming Blade / Steal Voice / Sound Cloud / Sound Wave",
     "tag": "Sonancer",
     "text": "Animal pacify, an instrument that can become a permanent +2 Will item, armour-shredding weapon buff, spellcaster silence, melee debuff aura, and a knockback shot."
    },
    {
     "name": "Call Wraith / Command Ethereal / Ethereal Form / Inhabit / Nightmare / Speak with the Dead",
     "tag": "Spiritualist",
     "text": "Summons a wraith, puppeteers ethereal undead, grants temporary ethereal form, possesses a construct/undead, curses a rival's XP long-term, and boosts a future Out of Game cast."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "Thaw of the Lich Lord": [
  {
   "title": "Lichdom — failing the cast",
   "blurb": "Lichdom — Necromancer / 20 / Out of Game. Only a wizard may cast it, tearing his soul from his body to animate his own corpse. Success makes him a Lich outright; failure is punishing and scales with how badly the roll was missed: A successful Lich is subject to a distinct rules package for the rest of the campaign — effectively a third alongside Demonic Pacts and Beastcrafter as a mutually-flavored \"wizard state\":",
   "rows": [
    {
     "name": "1–5",
     "text": "-1 level, -1 Health stat."
    },
    {
     "name": "6–10",
     "text": "-3 levels, -2 Health, -1 Will, 1 random permanent injury."
    },
    {
     "name": "11–15",
     "text": "-5 levels, -3 Health, -2 Will, 2 random permanent injuries."
    },
    {
     "name": "16–20",
     "text": "-8 levels, -6 Health, -2 Will, 3 random permanent injuries."
    },
    {
     "name": "21+",
     "text": "The wizard's soul is lost — unrecoverable by any means."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "The Perilous Dark": [
  {
   "title": "Solo & co-op toolkit",
   "blurb": "Also codifies house style for \"boss monsters\" (make them multi-step puzzles, not just high stat-lines — and make them immune to Mind Control) and gives explicit guidance that solo/co-op tables should run less crowded than a normal Frostgrave table, since the simple creature AI can't exploit terrain the way a player can.",
   "entries": [
    {
     "name": "Warband building",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Solo: build a warband as normal. Co-op: each player's wizard gets only 200gc and a max of 4 soldiers, so two players together land close to a solo warband's power."
    },
    {
     "name": "Objectives over treasure",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Place only 2 treasure tokens solo (3 co-op) in hard-to-reach spots, and shift most of the XP budget onto scenario-specific goals (e.g. \"+80 XP if the statue is destroyed\") to keep games from becoming a pure gold-count."
    },
    {
     "name": "New turn sequence",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Wizard Phase → Creature Phase I (creatures with Fight +5 or Health 20+) → Apprentice Phase → Creature Phase II (everyone else) → Soldier Phase — lets dangerous monsters act before the party's own soldiers."
    },
    {
     "name": "Updated creature AI",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Creatures activate in current-Health order, always move to engage the highest-level spellcaster if one move can do it, never intentionally pick a multi-combat, and all creatures get a flat +2 Will."
    },
    {
     "name": "Limited Duration spells",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Circle of Protection, Invisibility, Monstrous Form, and Wall: roll a die at the end of any turn they're active — on a 1–10 the spell ends early (replaces Wall's normal end-of-turn roll)."
    },
    {
     "name": "Target Points",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Instead of random movement, uncontrolled creatures head for a designer-nominated point (usually the objective) — makes the AI feel purposeful instead of aimless."
    },
    {
     "name": "Monster spawning",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Bespoke per-scenario spawn tables (not the core random-encounter table) tied to numbered spawn points, so the designer controls both what shows up and where."
    },
    {
     "name": "Clue Markers",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Treasure-token-like markers that reward investigation (an action + a roll on a scenario-specific clue table) — the game's investigation/mystery layer."
    },
    {
     "name": "Room Cards",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Draw a card to set up the next room only when a door is opened, so players never see the whole table at once; can also define room shape/doorway count for a lightweight dungeon crawl."
    },
    {
     "name": "Closely Connected Scenarios",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Rules for chaining scenarios back-to-back without a return to base: partial healing (+6 or half Health), no buying/selling/hiring, one Out of Game spell each."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Dungeon crawl systems",
   "blurb": "Two full worked examples ship with the book: The Vaults (a fixed 10-card dungeon deck with two objective rooms — a treasure hoard and a stairway down) and Dungeons Deep (the fully randomly-generated version, with its own room/encounter/treasure tables and the Gateway Home rule in action).",
   "entries": [
    {
     "name": "Dungeon Cards",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Like Room Cards but smaller and more numerous (10–12 sections instead of 3–5 rooms); the deck is split into a \"multi-exit\" pile and an \"objective room\" pile, then interleaved so objectives can't appear too early."
    },
    {
     "name": "Randomly Generated Dungeon",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "Each new room triggers three rolls — a room table, an encounter table, and a treasure/clue table — for a fully improvised dungeon; includes a Will TN20 \"search for a secret door\" rule to rescue dead-ended layouts."
    },
    {
     "name": "Gateway Room / Home",
     "tag": "",
     "text": "The randomly generated dungeon's designated exit: only spawns once 6+ rooms are revealed, guarded by a double encounter roll, and is the sole way off the table."
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}

